media: videobuf2-dma-sg: don't retry high orders a buffer needn't have

vb2_dma_sg_alloc_compacted() fills a buffer from the largest blocks it
can get. For each piece it starts again from the largest order that
fits what is left of the buffer, and steps down one order at a time
until an allocation succeeds. Each failed order above
PAGE_ALLOC_COSTLY_ORDER pays for direct compaction, direct reclaim and
compaction again before it fails. With memory fragmented, every piece
climbs back to the top and pays for every order again. Here a camera's
VIDIOC_REQBUFS, four 16.9 MB capture buffers on an IPU7 ISYS node,
spent 40 seconds in one call, at 12 seconds of system time after 15:

  __alloc_pages_slowpath
  __alloc_pages_direct_compact
  try_to_compact_pages
  compact_zone
  migrate_pages

The buffer is a scatter list, so its pages needn't be contiguous, and
iommu-dma's __iommu_dma_alloc_pages() already treats higher orders as
a convenience in the same way. So remember the order that last failed
and never ask for a higher one again, and ask for orders above 0 with
__GFP_NORETRY, keeping the allocator's full effort for single pages.
A buffer that fits in single pages still gets them, so this fails no
allocation that succeeded before.

--- a/drivers/media/common/videobuf2/videobuf2-dma-sg.c
+++ b/drivers/media/common/videobuf2/videobuf2-dma-sg.c
@@ -62,6 +62,8 @@ static int vb2_dma_sg_alloc_compacted(st
 {
 	unsigned int last_page = 0;
 	unsigned long size = buf->size;
+	gfp_t gfp = GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ZERO | __GFP_NOWARN | gfp_flags;
+	int max_order = MAX_PAGE_ORDER;
 
 	while (size > 0) {
 		struct page *pages;
@@ -72,11 +74,18 @@ static int vb2_dma_sg_alloc_compacted(st
 		/* Don't over allocate*/
 		if ((PAGE_SIZE << order) > size)
 			order--;
+		/* Don't ask again for an order that has already failed */
+		order = min(order, max_order);
 
 		pages = NULL;
 		while (!pages) {
-			pages = alloc_pages(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ZERO |
-					__GFP_NOWARN | gfp_flags, order);
+			/*
+			 * The buffer is scattered anyway, so higher orders are
+			 * a convenience: ask for them without retrying, and
+			 * keep the allocator's full effort for single pages.
+			 */
+			pages = alloc_pages(order ? gfp | __GFP_NORETRY : gfp,
+					    order);
 			if (pages)
 				break;
 
@@ -86,6 +95,7 @@ static int vb2_dma_sg_alloc_compacted(st
 				return -ENOMEM;
 			}
 			order--;
+			max_order = order;
 		}
 
 		split_page(pages, order);
